{"qid":"Q894030","names":["Bornu Empire"],"title":"Bornu Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Bornu Empire was the western successor of the ancient Kanem–Bornu state, centred on the Kanuri heartland west of Lake Chad after the Sayfawa dynasty relocated from Kanem in the late 14th century. A Muslim trans-Saharan trading power that exchanged slaves, ivory and hides for horses and firearms, it reached its height under Mai Idris Alooma (r. c. 1564–1596), who imported Ottoman muskets and military advisers. Bornu survived the 19th-century Fulani jihad under the new dynasty of the scholar-statesman al-Kanemi before falling to the warlord Rabih az-Zubayr in 1893 and then to European colonial partition.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Borders attested from Cliopatria (CC BY 4.0); polity history from the cited mainstream references.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Encyclopædia Britannica — Kanem-Bornu","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kanem-Bornu"},{"label":"UNESCO — General History of Africa","url":"https://en.unesco.org/general-history-africa"}],"britannica":"place/Bornu-historical-kingdom-and-emirate-Nigeria","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kanem-Bornu Empire","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Umar I Idrismi","nativeName":"Umar I Idrismi","roman":"Umar I Idrismi","start":1385,"end":1387,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Sa'id","nativeName":"Sa'id","roman":"Said","start":1387,"end":1388,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Muhammad II Manza","nativeName":"Muhammad II Manza","roman":"Muhammad II Manza","start":1388,"end":1389,"image":null,